    {
      "number": 5,
      "id": "I_kwDOSIVa8M8AAAABBNxvVw",
      "title": "do we need to focus on user to agent communication in the use cases?",
      "url": "https://github.com/tireddy2/agentic-ai-ucreq2/issues/5",
      "state": "CLOSED",
      "author": "zaheduzzaman",
      "authorAssociation": "COLLABORATOR",
      "assignees": [],
      "labels": [],
      "body": "There has been lots of discussions on whether and how much IETF protocol can do when it comes to Human interaction. This becomes a controversial topic the moment we touch on trust model, how much human should be in the look. Hence, we need to see what is the minimum use case we have when we have Human in the loop. And if the current use cases can be converted to the agent to agent communication. ",
      "createdAt": "2026-05-04T12:22:50Z",
      "updatedAt": "2026-05-06T13:03:32Z",
      "closedAt": "2026-05-06T13:03:32Z",
      "comments": [
        {
          "author": "tireddy2",
          "authorAssociation": "OWNER",
          "body": "Agreed, minimized text related to interaction with human.  ",
          "createdAt": "2026-05-06T13:03:32Z",
          "updatedAt": "2026-05-06T13:03:32Z"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "number": 8,
      "id": "I_kwDOSIVa8M8AAAABBlsdeA",
      "title": "use case:  authority proxy",
      "url": "https://github.com/tireddy2/agentic-ai-ucreq2/issues/8",
      "state": "CLOSED",
      "author": "zaheduzzaman",
      "authorAssociation": "COLLABORATOR",
      "assignees": [],
      "labels": [],
      "body": "A authority proxy can be viewed as a relationship where an agent acts on behalf of a Human or Legal entity.\n\nIs that an use case or just a better name for a relationship in the existing usecases.",
      "createdAt": "2026-05-07T19:58:34Z",
      "updatedAt": "2026-05-09T06:06:40Z",
      "closedAt": "2026-05-09T06:06:40Z",
      "comments": [
        {
          "author": "tireddy2",
          "authorAssociation": "OWNER",
          "body": "It is already covered in the draft, for instance, In A.1 the agent acts as an authority proxy for the user, invoking tools on the user's behalf. In B.1, the orchestrator acts as an authority proxy for the user when delegating to subagents\n",
          "createdAt": "2026-05-09T06:06:40Z",
          "updatedAt": "2026-05-09T06:06:40Z"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "number": 9,
      "id": "I_kwDOSIVa8M8AAAABBluXLg",
      "title": "Add protocol considerations for recursive delegation",
      "url": "https://github.com/tireddy2/agentic-ai-ucreq2/issues/9",
      "state": "CLOSED",
      "author": "zaheduzzaman",
      "authorAssociation": "COLLABORATOR",
      "assignees": [],
      "labels": [],
      "body": " If Agent A delegates to Agent B, and Agent B delegates to Agent C, the protocol must ensure that Agent C is still bound by the original constraints set by the human owner of Agent A. This requires transferable but verifiable credentials, ensuring the security context is passed down the chain without degradation.\n\nI am not sure we have this captured directly. ",
      "createdAt": "2026-05-07T20:04:16Z",
      "updatedAt": "2026-05-09T06:19:11Z",
      "closedAt": "2026-05-09T06:19:11Z",
      "comments": [
        {
          "author": "tireddy2",
          "authorAssociation": "OWNER",
          "body": "Yes, check https://github.com/tireddy2/agentic-ai-ucreq2/pull/7. ",
          "createdAt": "2026-05-09T06:07:09Z",
          "updatedAt": "2026-05-09T06:19:10Z"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
